course: dictionary
lesson: 11271
title: Wriggle: twist, slip, and dodge
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: To wriggle means to twist and turn your body quickly from side to side. «The worm wriggled in my hand.» The word tells you the worm was twisting and turning, not sliding smoothly.
explain: Wriggle has a clever meaning too. To wriggle out of something means to avoid doing it by being sly or making excuses. «The sly boy wriggled out of trouble with a story» means he got away from being told off by being clever.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «It wriggled free, fell, and broke its back leg» shows the guinea pig was twisting hard to escape when it was lifted. Swap in twist or slip and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Wriggle can be a verb (wriggle, wriggles, wriggled, wriggling) or a noun (a wriggle).
fact: «Wriggled» means twisted and turned quickly from side to side.
fact: «Wriggle free» means to twist until you get loose.
fact: «Wriggle out of» means to avoid doing something by being clever or making excuses.
fact: A wriggle is a twisting movement, as in «She gave a wriggle and sat up».
exercise: «The worm wriggled in my hand.» What does wriggled mean? | Stood very still | Went to sleep | *Twisted and turned quickly | Flew up into the air
exercise: «He wriggled out of cleaning his room by making excuses.» What does wriggled out of mean here? | Cleaned it very carefully | Hid inside the wardrobe | Threw everything away | *Avoided it by being sly
exercise: Find and copy the word that means twisted and turned from side to side: «The bored children wriggled on the carpet.» | carpet | *wriggled | children | sat
test: «The guinea pig wriggled free and fell.» Wriggled free means: | Ran into a box | Stayed on the table | *Got loose by twisting | Went to sleep
test: Which sentence uses wriggle as a noun? | He wriggled into his coat | *The dog gave a happy wriggle | They are wriggling about | She wriggles on her seat
exam: «The sly boy wriggled out of trouble with a story.» What does wriggled out of mean? | *Avoided trouble by being clever | Twisted his body in pain | Climbed out of a window | Ran straight to the teacher
